Estimate touch release velocity with Flutter's velocity trackers, with Flutter's tests as the oracle.

What: replaces `gestures.rs`'s private `VelocityTracker` (and its `quadratic_velocity_at_newest` fit) with a
near-verbatim Rust port of Flutter 3.50.1's `velocity_tracker.dart` (`gestures/velocity_tracker.rs`: `VelocityTracker`,
`IOSScrollViewFlingVelocityTracker`, `MacOSScrollViewFlingVelocityTracker` as one tracker with a kind) and
`lsq_solver.dart` (`gestures/lsq_solver.rs`: `LeastSquaresSolver`, `PolynomialFit`). Adds the public
`VelocityTrackerKind` and `GestureTuning::velocity_tracker`, which picks the kind (`IOSScrollViewFling` in
`GestureTuning::default()`, GPUI's iOS-flavoured defaults, as `ScrollPhysics::ios()`; Parallax's platform table
holds the other rows of Flutter's `ScrollBehavior.velocityTrackerBuilder`). `get_velocity_estimate` keeps Flutter's
`VelocityEstimate.offset` (the distance the estimate spans) for the recognizer's fling-distance check. The recognizer
calls `add_position` where it pushed samples and `get_velocity(now)` on release; the tracker's own 40 ms "pointer
stopped" check replaces the recognizer's. Ports
Flutter's `velocity_tracker_test.dart` (with the `velocity_tracker_data.dart` traces) and `lsq_solver_test.dart` as
table-driven in-crate tests, one row per Flutter test, named after it. The two upstream velocity tests stay, on the
`LeastSquares` kind.

Why: upstream's tracker approximates only Flutter's base least-squares tracker. Against Flutter's tests it passes the
two recorded traces and the lsq rows it can be asked about, but has no iOS or macOS strategy, so it fails "Makes
consistent velocity estimates with consistent velocity" and has no twin for the two iOS assertion tests; it also
differs outside the tests (an endpoint estimate from 2 samples where Flutter reports zero, a 1e-12 determinant cutoff
in seconds). Flutter's trackers are tuned against each platform's native scroll views, so Parallax ports them whole
(docs/velocity-tracking.md). The tests reach private recognizer code, which GPUI's test platform can't, so they live
in the crate. Not platform-specific code, so no cfg; the per-platform choice is data in `GestureTuning`.

Code under `gpui-pre/src/gestures/` is ported from Flutter (BSD-3-Clause, header on each file, ledger in Parallax's
third_party/flutter/PORTED.md).

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

